    <body>
      <sec id="article-131531.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Childhood obesity is one of the most prevalent chronic conditions in pediatric populations and has escalated into a global epidemic. Genetic, physiological, environmental, and socioeconomic factors contribute to&#x000a0;the development of this condition. Childhood obesity increases the likelihood of adult obesity and places affected individuals at risk for serious short- and long-term adverse health outcomes and comorbidities. In the U.S., childhood obesity rates have more than tripled since the 1960s, with prevalence increasing with age.</p>
        <p>Diagnosis is based on body mass index percentiles adjusted for sex and age, along with evaluation for comorbidities such as dyslipidemia, hypertension, and insulin resistance. Management focuses on lifestyle modification through dietary changes, increased physical activity, and behavioral intervention. Pharmacologic and surgical therapies are reserved for severe cases with significant health risks. Delayed or inadequate prevention, diagnosis, and treatment contribute to pediatric comorbidities that frequently persist into adulthood.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals&#x000a0;is designed to enhance learners' competence in evaluating and managing childhood obesity. Participants will deepen their understanding of the condition's epidemiology, etiology, diagnosis, evaluation, treatment, and complications. Greater proficiency will equip clinicians to collaborate fruitfully as part of an interprofessional team caring for&#x000a0;young individuals with this condition.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify patient-specific factors contributing to obesity at a young age.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select the appropriate&#x000a0;evaluation tools to grade obesity and determine the onset of complications in pediatric patients.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Create a personalized approach to managing pediatric obesity and preventing its complications, noting patient-specific contributing factors and diagnostic results.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional team to educate, treat, and monitor pediatric patients with obesity to prevent complications and improve health outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=131531&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=131531">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Obesity is a complex and widespread health issue that often persists into adolescence. The prevalence of obesity in children aged 6 to 11 years increased from 4.2% between 1963 and 1965 to&#x000a0;15.3% between 1999 and 2000. According to the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey, approximately 20% of children in the U.S. have obesity, while another 16% have a body mass index (BMI) in the overweight category.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Obesity is defined as having a BMI greater than or equal to the 95th percentile for age and sex in children 2 years and older. To minimize stigma and reflect current clinical practice,&#x000a0;obesity is now categorized as class 1, 2, or 3 instead of being labeled "severe" or "morbid." Classes are defined based on percentiles of BMI for age<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>and sex from standardized growth charts, such as those from the&#x000a0;Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)&#x000a0;or the World Health Organization&#x000a0;(WHO).&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Class&#x000a0;1 obesity: BMI greater than or equal to the 95th percentile but less than 120% of the 95th percentile</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Class&#x000a0;2 obesity: BMI greater than or equal to 120% but less than 140% of the 95th percentile</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Class&#x000a0;3 obesity: BMI greater than or equal to 140% of the 95th percentile.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>For children younger than 2 years, the CDC recommends using the WHO weight-for-length charts, which are age- and sex-specific, rather than the BMI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In 2025, a global commission on obesity, comprising representatives from various medical specialties and nations, proposed a comprehensive redefinition of obesity. The new criteria incorporate BMI along with at least&#x000a0;1 additional anthropometric measurement, such as waist circumference or waist-to-hip ratio, coupled with evidence of organ system dysfunction or functional limitations in activities of daily living. The commission classified children and adults with excess adiposity but no signs of organ dysfunction or functional impairment as having preclinical obesity, emphasizing the importance of early intervention to prevent significant health consequences.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Child obesity rates in the U.S. have more than tripled since the 1960s, and prevalence increases with age.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Failure to prevent, diagnose, and treat&#x000a0;this condition&#x000a0;results in&#x000a0;pediatric comorbidities that often&#x000a0;continue into&#x000a0;adulthood.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Obesity is a multifactorial condition, resulting from a complex interplay among genetic, biological, environmental, socioeconomic, and cultural drivers. Genetics and biology are predetermined, while other&#x000a0;contributors may be modified. Modifiable factors include family behaviors related to eating, sleeping, and exercise, access to healthy food in schools and communities, availability of safe places for physical activity, and adverse childhood experiences.</p>
        <p>Weight gain occurs when energy intake chronically exceeds energy expenditure, with both measured in calories, eventually leading to obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r5">[5]</xref> Genetic determinants play a lesser role. Shifting food preferences in recent decades have been influenced by marketing and the increased availability of high-calorie, processed foods, such as fast food and sugar-sweetened beverages.&#x000a0;The widespread consumption of hyperpalatable, energy-dense foods, combined with larger portion sizes and frequent snacking, has been strongly associated with the rising prevalence of obesity in industrialized nations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Decreased physical activity and increased screen time&#x02014;via smartphones, computers, televisions, and video games&#x02014;contribute to the problem.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r7">[7]</xref> Studies show obesity rates rose during the COVID-19 pandemic when school closures led to more screen and sedentary time, replacing active play and physical activities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>Feeding patterns are culturally influenced, with children often modeling their behavior after their parents and families from a young age. Research indicates that higher parental education and regular family meals have a positive influence on children's healthy food choices. Families eating together, exposing children to a variety of nutritious foods, and avoiding television during meals foster a positive food environment that reduces the likelihood of obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r9">[9]</xref> The amount of time a child spends watching television and playing video games correlates with increased distracted eating and obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Other critical risk factors may predispose individuals to obesity. Perinatal and postnatal conditions that increase risk include elevated maternal BMI during pregnancy, large-for-gestational-age birth weight, rapid weight gain in infancy and early childhood, and the contrast between breastfeeding, a protective factor, and formula feeding. Environmental chemicals, microbiota, early antibiotic use, and adverse life experiences are also considered potential risk factors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Alterations in the gut microbiome may contribute to unhealthy weight gain, as dysbiosis increases the production of short-chain fatty acids associated with a higher risk of obesity, type 2 diabetes (T2D), and metabolic syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r14">[14]</xref> Prenatal exposure to endocrine-disrupting chemicals such as bisphenol A and dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane has been hypothesized to increase obesity risk by modulating estrogen receptor activity and altering metabolic programming.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Polygenic and monogenic obesity, though uncommon, merit consideration. Polygenic obesity results from the interaction of several polygenic variants with environmental factors, increasing the likelihood of obesity. Further genetic and molecular studies are needed to determine the clinical significance of these genetic variations. Monogenic obesity is severe and has an early onset, typically caused by an autosomal recessive mutation in the leptin-melanocortin pathway. Deficiencies in&#x000a0;melanocortin 4 receptors (MC4R) and proopiomelanocortin increase food-seeking behaviors in children, while leptin and leptin receptor deficiencies result in early-onset extreme obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Genetic disorders associated with obesity include Prader-Willi (PWS), Alstrom, Bardet-Biedl (BBS), and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndromes, as well as Albright hereditary osteodystrophy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Secondary etiologies of obesity, such as endocrine, neurologic, psychological, and drug-induced causes, should be considered when the onset of obesity is sudden or unexpected. The resulting hormonal imbalances affecting satiety and food-seeking behaviors are the&#x000a0;likely mechanisms promoting the condition in these cases.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The prevalence of obesity in children aged 6 to 11 years&#x000a0;rose from 4.2% between 1963 and 1965 to&#x000a0;15.3% between 1999 and 2000.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Data from the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey for 2015&#x000a0;to 2016 show that the trend has continued. According to this survey, the prevalence of obesity in the U.S.&#x000a0;among children and teens is 18.5%.&#x000a0;The rate among adolescents&#x000a0;aged 12&#x000a0;to 19 is higher at 20.6%.</p>
        <p>Approximately 13.5 million American adolescents struggle with obesity. Disparities exist across racial and ethnic groups, with non-Hispanic Black and Mexican American children exhibiting higher rates of obesity than non-Hispanic White children. Factors such as parental educational level and income, access to healthy food options, and availability of safe physical activity opportunities contribute to disparities in obesity prevalence. Additionally, children with disabilities, including autism and intellectual disabilities, are at higher risk&#x000a0;of developing obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Appetite and satiety are controlled through neuroendocrine feedback mechanisms. Gastrointestinal hormones, such as ghrelin, stimulate appetite, while glucagon-like peptide 1 (GLP-1), peptide YY (PYY), cholecystokinin, and vagal neural feedback inhibit appetite and promote satiety.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Adipose tissue contributes to energy balance through the regulation of adiponectin and leptin, which signal satiety. These hormones provide feedback to the arcuate nucleus in the hypothalamus, which, in turn, sends behavioral and autonomic outputs to the solitary tract nucleus in the brainstem, stimulating gastrointestinal hormone secretion.</p>
        <p>Neuropeptides in the brain, including PYY, agouti-related peptide, and orexin, stimulate appetite, whereas melanocortin and &#x003b1;-melanocyte-stimulating hormones promote satiety.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r22">[22]</xref> A genetic deficiency of the melanocortin 4 receptor (MC4R) has been associated with increased food-seeking behavior.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Lack of sleep has been shown to&#x000a0;decrease&#x000a0;levels of leptin, a hormone that signals fullness, and increase levels of ghrelin, a hormone that stimulates hunger. Ghrelin, the "hunger hormone," activates PYY and agouti-related peptide, which stimulate appetite.</p>
        <p>The neuroendocrine interaction between the gut, adipose tissue, and brain maintains the balance between appetite stimulation and adiposity. Mutations in genes involved in the secretion of these hormones are of interest in epigenetic modifications and may represent potential therapeutic targets for these conditions.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Evaluation of patients with obesity begins with a comprehensive medical history and a careful physical examination to identify the etiology and any comorbid conditions. This process typically occurs during a well-child visit at the medical home but may also take place during problem-focused appointments. The clinician should first inquire whether the family or patient has concerns about the child&#x02019;s growth or weight. History-taking should begin with reviewing prenatal factors, such as maternal weight gain during pregnancy or gestational diabetes, which may increase the likelihood of obesity. Perinatal history should include questions about intrauterine growth retardation followed by rapid catch-up growth or whether the child was large for gestational age at birth.</p>
        <p>A nonjudgmental approach is essential when probing the details of a child&#x02019;s nutritional intake and energy expenditure. Reviewing family feeding patterns, meal composition, snacking habits, and screen time can help uncover the cause of excessive weight gain. A 24-hour diet recall, which lists the servings of fruits, vegetables, and high-carbohydrate and high-calorie foods in meals, snacks, and beverages, provides valuable information. Additionally, noting the duration and type of physical activity can help assess energy expenditure.</p>
        <p>Reviewing height and weight growth charts for abrupt changes in weight, height, and BMI trajectories may suggest an underlying etiology. Extreme appetite and food-seeking behaviors are observed in some genetic conditions.</p>
        <p>A family history of obesity and obesity-related comorbid conditions can provide further insights. Genetic susceptibility may be present, particularly if family members have experienced severe obesity requiring bariatric surgery, or&#x000a0;the condition affects multiple generations. The presence of diabetes, hypertension, or cardiovascular disease in a family member increases the risk of a child developing similar conditions. Parental obesity is another risk factor, likely influenced by the interplay of genetics, shared environment, and social determinants of health. Clinicians should help families understand that childhood obesity increases the likelihood of developing several chronic diseases.</p>
        <p>History&#x000a0;may provide vital clues to less common, secondary causes of obesity. Significant head trauma preceding the onset of sudden weight gain may suggest a hypothalamic cause. Symptoms such as easy bruising, muscle weakness, fatigue, and central obesity may indicate Cushing syndrome, while cold intolerance, dry skin, and anterior neck swelling suggest hypothyroidism.</p>
        <p>Complete leptin deficiency is a rare autosomal recessive disorder that causes severe obesity, impaired satiety, intense hyperphagia, persistent food-seeking behavior, and recurrent bacterial infections.&#x000a0;This condition also leads to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, which results in delayed puberty.</p>
        <p>However,&#x000a0;less than 1% of children with obesity are diagnosed with an endocrine cause, despite many families believing weight gain is due to a hormone imbalance.&#x000a0;Reviewing medications linked to weight gain, such as steroids, antipsychotics (eg, risperidone), and antiepileptics (eg, valproate), is essential when evaluating for drug-induced obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Signs and symptoms elicited on history that&#x000a0;suggest&#x000a0;the presence of comorbidities include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Diabetes: Polyuria and polydipsia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pseudotumor cerebri: Headache and vision changes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Blount disease and slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE): Limp with diminished internal rotation of the hip and associated hip or knee pain</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gallbladder disease and&#x000a0;metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD): Abdominal pain, vomiting, and jaundice</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Behavioral or psychological problems: Anxiety and depression, worsening school performance, and experiences of bullying</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Asthma and obstructive sleep apnea (OSA): Shortness of breath, snoring, and daytime sleepiness or fatigue</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS): Irregular menses, hirsutism, and acne</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Behavioral and emotional concerns should be assessed using validated screening tools. Instruments such as the Patient Health Questionnaire 9 (PHQ-9) can aid in identifying psychological comorbidities.</p>
        <p>Physical examination begins with&#x000a0;measuring&#x000a0;height, weight, and blood pressure and calculating the BMI, which has the following formula:</p>
        <p>BMI = Weight in kg &#x000f7; (Height in m)<sup>2</sup></p>
        <p>Blood pressure should be measured with an appropriately sized cuff to ensure accuracy.&#x000a0;A head-to-toe examination should follow to identify potential causes and complications of obesity.</p>
        <p>Facial and body dysmorphisms may indicate underlying genetic disorders. For example, almond-shaped eyes, hypogonadism, and small hands and feet are characteristic of PWS. Other features, including hypotonia, upward-slanting eyes, a short neck, and transverse palmar creases, are associated with Down syndrome. A round face, central obesity, and excess fatty tissue on the upper back, particularly between the shoulders, may suggest Cushing syndrome. Cognitive impairment may also point to a genetic etiology. Short stature is commonly linked to hormone deficiencies and Albright hereditary osteodystrophy.</p>
        <p>Ocular findings such as papilledema (possibly&#x000a0;due to pseudotumor cerebri), retinal degeneration, and nystagmus (associated with BBS) should prompt specialist evaluation. Dermatologic changes, including acanthosis nigricans, characterized by thickened, darkened skin on the nape of the neck, along with acne and hirsutism, may suggest insulin resistance or PCOS. Sexual maturity ratings may reveal premature adrenarche in girls with obesity.</p>
        <p>Clinicians should also assess for dyspnea or wheezing, which may indicate asthma, and for abdominal tenderness or hepatomegaly, which may indicate MASLD. Limited hip or knee mobility may be a sign of SCFE and Blount disease, respectively.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Primary care clinicians typically diagnose obesity during health maintenance visits. The growth chart not only reveals the onset and severity of&#x000a0;this condition but also provides clues about its potential causes. The BMI trajectory helps differentiate between a gradual increase during sensitive growth periods, such as infancy, childhood, and adolescence, and an abrupt acceleration. Sudden BMI changes may signal personal or family stress or medical issues such as tumors, trauma, hypothyroidism,&#x000a0;and medication use.</p>
        <p>Comparing weight and height trajectories can be particularly informative. Both weight and height typically increase in a linear pattern in a young child consuming excess calories. However, disproportionate weight gain may signal insufficient physical activity and a predominantly sedentary lifestyle. A deceleration in linear growth over time may point to endocrine issues, such as hypothyroidism.</p>
        <p>Laboratory investigation should focus on the early detection of comorbidities, as the risk increases with a child&#x02019;s age and the severity of obesity. The prevalence of comorbidities also varies by race and ethnicity. Black and Hispanic youth have higher rates of prediabetes and T2D, while Hispanic children experience higher rates of MASLD. These differences are likely influenced by socioeconomic and environmental factors rather than genetic ones.</p>
        <p>In children with obesity, evaluation for lipid abnormalities, prediabetes, T2D, and hepatic dysfunction should begin at age 10. Recommended assessments include a fasting lipid panel, fasting glucose, and alanine transaminase every 2 years. Aspartate transaminase may also be measured, but alanine transaminase is more specific to the liver and serves as a better marker of hepatocellular injury related to hepatic steatosis. If fasting glucose is elevated, follow-up testing with hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) or a 2-hour plasma glucose level after an oral glucose tolerance test may be warranted.</p>
        <p>In children aged 2 to 9 years, routine screening for lipid abnormalities is generally unnecessary but may be indicated in the presence of a positive family history. The risk of prediabetes, T2D, and MASLD is lower in this age group.&#x000a0;Therefore,&#x000a0;testing is not universally recommended. However, screening for glucose abnormalities or liver dysfunction is appropriate when physical findings, such as acanthosis nigricans, are present, or a family history of diabetes is elicited.</p>
        <p>Testing for levels of luteinizing hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, and testosterone should be performed when PCOS is suspected. A thyroid hormone assessment is indicated&#x000a0;for concerns about&#x000a0;thyroid function&#x000a0;and serum cortisol levels to diagnose Cushing syndrome.</p>
        <p>Other studies may also be considered when clinically indicated. Imaging&#x000a0;modalities include radiographs of the hip and knee joints to look for signs of&#x000a0;SCFE&#x000a0;and Blount disease, as well as ultrasound of the abdomen and pelvis to search for features consistent with PCOS. Brain computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging may be used to assess for&#x000a0;hypothalamic tumors.&#x000a0;Polysomnography is used to confirm or exclude OSA, while pulmonary function testing assists in diagnosing asthma. A lumbar puncture with measurement of cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure may be performed to evaluate for pseudotumor cerebri.</p>
        <p>The 2025 Global Commission, an expert panel convened by The Lancet Diabetes&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;Endocrinology and composed of approximately 56 leading specialists and patient representatives, published a report identifying 13 diagnostic criteria that indicate obesity-related organ dysfunction. The included clinical&#x000a0;features are as follows:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Symptoms of increased intracranial pressure, including vision loss and recurrent headaches</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>OSA</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Breathlessness or wheezing attributable to excess weight</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypertension</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Presence of hyperglycemia, hypertriglyceridemia, and low high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Elevated liver enzymes consistent with MASLD</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Microalbuminuria</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrent urinary incontinence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>PCOS</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrent pain, tripping, or falling due to pes planus or lower-limb malalignment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrent pain or reduced mobility associated with tibia vara</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>SCFE</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Limitations in day-to-day function or mobility</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These criteria underscore the multisystem impact of obesity in children and adolescents. Recognizing early signs of organ dysfunction allows for timely intervention and prevention of long-term complications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Lifestyle and Behavioral Interventions</bold>
</p>
        <p>Evidence supports treating obesity and its comorbidities to achieve weight loss, prevent further weight gain, and improve related complications. Guidelines do not recommend a "watchful waiting" approach. Clinicians must document the presence of obesity and clearly explain to caregivers the importance of addressing&#x000a0;this problem while avoiding stigmatizing the child. Asking for permission to discuss the child's BMI and weight can help facilitate this conversation. Labels like "obese child" must be avoided, and neutral terms, such as "unhealthy weight" or "gaining too much weight for age or height," must be used instead. The primary focus should remain on the child's health.</p>
        <p>Behavioral change is the cornerstone of managing pediatric obesity. Family-based interventions should&#x000a0;center on reducing BMI by limiting the intake of sweetened beverages and fast food, increasing fruit and vegetable consumption, and promoting physical activity. Clinicians should provide clear recommendations on healthy food choices and portion sizes while also encouraging daily physical activity. Simply advising "eat less and exercise more" is too simplistic. Families should be encouraged to plan meals that take children's preferences into account while ensuring a balanced, nutrient-rich diet.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>No single specific diet has been proven superior for weight loss. Approaches such as the "traffic light diet plan" can help families make healthy choices that respect cultural values and dietary practices.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r26">[26]</xref> Another commonly recommended strategy is the "5-2-1-0" guideline: 5 daily servings of fruits and vegetables, no more than 2 hours of screen time, at least 1 hour of physical activity, and 0 sugar-sweetened beverages. The American Heart Association advises limiting added sugars to no more than 25 g (6 tsp) per day and avoiding sugar-sweetened beverages altogether.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>Primary care clinicians can support behavior change by encouraging the removal of unhealthy foods from the home, use of food diaries, positive reinforcement, and goal setting for nutrition and physical activity. Clinicians must also recognize families&#x000a0;facing food insecurity or living in environments without access to safe play spaces or adequate food resources.</p>
        <p>Creating a healthy food environment at home involves&#x000a0;incorporating&#x000a0;nutritious options for meals and snacks and avoiding screen time during meals. Children often model the behaviors of adults in the household, and positive family-level changes can significantly support both the prevention and treatment of obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r29">[29]</xref> Motivational interviewing can help assess the family's understanding of obesity-related health risks and guide them in adopting strategies that reflect their values and beliefs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;Involving both patients and their parents in goal-setting fosters sustainable and beneficial behavioral changes.</p>
        <p>The Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans recommend that children aged 6 to 17 years engage in at least 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous physical activity each day. Children aged 3 to 5 years should be physically active throughout the day to support growth and development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r31">[31]</xref> Both aerobic and resistance exercises have been shown to improve the metabolic profile of children and reduce the risk of cardiovascular events.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r32">[32]</xref> Increasing physical activity can involve&#x000a0;routines such as walking to school, participating in sports, engaging in unstructured outdoor play, and exercising with family members during leisure time. Encouraging family-wide physical activity is more effective in promoting a healthy, active lifestyle than focusing solely on the child.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends limiting screen time to less than 1 hour&#x000a0;daily for children aged 2 to 5 years and 2 hours&#x000a0;daily&#x000a0;for older children. For children&#x000a0;younger than 2 years, no screen time is recommended, except for video chats with friends and family. Increased screen time is linked to lower levels of physical activity.</p>
        <p>Primary care clinicians, often working with limited resources and time, may find it challenging to provide the intensive behavior and lifestyle interventions recommended for obesity treatment. A structured weight management plan typically involves motivational interviewing and referrals to a registered dietitian or nutrition specialist.&#x000a0;The next step often involves a comprehensive, multifaceted approach that includes more frequent and intensive behavior change sessions, typically delivered by an interprofessional team, with up to 22 sessions over a 3- to 12-month period. In some cases, patients may benefit from tertiary care interventions that include expert advice on nutrition and exercise, behavior counseling, pharmacologic therapy, and consideration of bariatric surgery.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pharmacologic Treatment</bold>
</p>
        <p>Evidence does not support weight loss medication use as a standalone therapy.&#x000a0;However,&#x000a0;this approach&#x000a0;may be recommended as an adjunct when intensive behavioral interventions have not been successful. Many&#x000a0;agents used for weight loss in adults have not been approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for pediatric use, and ongoing studies continue to evaluate their efficacy and safety in this population.</p>
        <p>Orlistat, approved by the FDA for use in children with obesity&#x000a0;aged 12 years and older, is a pancreatic lipase inhibitor that promotes mild to moderate weight loss by inhibiting fat absorption. Although effective,&#x000a0;the use of this drug may be limited by gastrointestinal adverse effects, including flatulence, fecal urgency, and steatorrhea. The recommended dose is 120 mg orally,&#x000a0;3 times daily.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>Metformin is FDA-approved for treating T2D in children aged 10 years and older. While not approved for weight loss,&#x000a0;this medication has demonstrated mild-to-moderate weight reduction in pediatric patients receiving it for T2D. Metformin is also used in managing PCOS by decreasing hepatic glucose production, reducing intestinal glucose absorption, and enhancing insulin sensitivity. The starting dose is 500 mg orally, once or twice daily, and may be titrated to a maximum of 2,500 mg per day.</p>
        <p>GLP-1 receptor agonists help reduce hunger by slowing gastric emptying and&#x000a0;influencing central nervous system&#x000a0;targets. Liraglutide, injected daily, is FDA-approved for children&#x000a0;with obesity aged 12 years and older. Exenatide, administered weekly, is approved for treating children with T2D&#x000a0;aged 10 to 17 years. Common adverse effects of GLP-1 medications include nausea and vomiting.</p>
        <p>MC4R agonists, such as setmelanotide, are FDA-approved for children aged 6 years and older with rare genetic conditions, including proopiomelanocortin and leptin deficiency. Setmelanotide, which is injected daily, decreases appetite. Adverse effects may include nausea and injection site reactions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>Phentermine and topiramate extended-release capsules are FDA-approved for weight loss in adults. Recent data have shown significant weight loss and improved lipid profiles in adolescents.&#x000a0;However, this combination is not currently&#x000a0;recommended for use in children. Topiramate reduces appetite but is associated with cognitive slowing and teratogenicity. Consequently, this medication should be prescribed with caution, particularly in older adolescents.</p>
        <p>Lisdexamfetamine is a stimulant sometimes used off-label in adults with obesity.&#x000a0;This agent is approved for treating binge eating disorder in adults and attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder in pediatric patients. Lisdexamfetamine may be beneficial when a teen requires treatment for&#x000a0;both&#x000a0;attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder and obesity.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>Weight loss surgery can be a safe and effective option for pediatric patients with severe obesity. Candidates typically have a BMI&#x000a0;of at least 35 kg/m&#x000b2; or 120% of the 95th percentile for age and sex, whichever is lower. The American Pediatric Surgical Association recommends surgical management for adolescents who have near-complete skeletal maturity and are at risk for medical complications related to obesity after failing intensive behavioral interventions in an interprofessional weight management program lasting at least 6 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>Bariatric and metabolic surgeries, such as laparoscopic vertical sleeve gastrectomy and Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, are options for pediatric patients with severe obesity.&#x000a0;Laparoscopic adjustable gastric band procedures are approved only for patients aged 18 years and older and are associated with lower long-term success and higher complication rates. Referral to comprehensive surgical centers with experience in caring for young patients and their families is recommended. Although no specific minimum age has been established, most bariatric procedures are performed in adolescents aged 13 years or older. Surgery may still be appropriate in selected cases involving younger children with severe comorbidities. Evaluation for bariatric surgery must consider chronological age, emotional maturity, and the patient&#x02019;s capacity to adhere to necessary lifestyle changes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r37">[37]</xref></p>
        <p>Similar to adults, pediatric bariatric patients&#x000a0;may experience significant reductions in BMI and improvement or resolution of many obesity-related comorbid conditions, including hypertension, T2D, dyslipidemia, and weight-related quality of life. Postoperative complications&#x000a0;like nausea and dehydration are typically minor, but up to a quarter of patients may require additional procedures within the next 5 years.&#x000a0;Close follow-up and long-term monitoring for micronutrient deficiencies and malabsorption are essential, with vitamin and mineral supplementation often required to prevent or correct these complications.</p>
        <p>Before&#x000a0;receiving bariatric and metabolic surgery, patients and their families must undergo a thorough evaluation and education regarding the surgical risks and benefits, ensuring they can make informed decisions. The family must understand that lifestyle modifications are essential after bariatric procedures and that surgery is not a "quick fix." The interprofessional surgical team should conduct a comprehensive physiological and psychosocial assessment to determine whether the patient is a suitable candidate for surgery.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Clinicians should evaluate for uncommon secondary causes and recognize medical conditions associated with obesity. Attenuated height growth velocity suggests endocrine causes like hypothyroidism, hypothalamic tumor, growth hormone deficiency, and Cushing syndrome. A history of significant head trauma&#x000a0;requires ruling out&#x000a0;central nervous system&#x000a0;injury&#x000a0;as the cause of&#x000a0;hypothalamic obesity. In such cases, an abrupt change in the trajectory of weight gain is usually observed following the inciting event.</p>
        <p>Hyperphagia, atypical facial features, and neurodevelopmental delays accompanied by obesity may indicate genetic disorders. DNA methylation studies&#x000a0;can confirm PWS. Ocular abnormalities, such as retinal dystrophy and nystagmus, are associated with&#x000a0;BBS and Alstrom syndrome and warrant further investigation. Developmental delays in the presence of severe obesity should raise suspicion for rare conditions, such as congenital leptin or proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 1 (PCSK1) deficiencies, which&#x000a0;may be identified by measuring serum leptin, proinsulin, and insulin levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r38">[38]</xref> MC4R deficiency is a cause of early-onset pediatric obesity and is diagnosed through molecular genetic studies.</p>
        <p>Idiopathic intracranial hypertension, also known as pseudotumor cerebri, is a&#x000a0;rare but&#x000a0;serious neurological&#x000a0;condition most commonly affecting young women with obesity or overweight. Obesity&#x000a0;does not cause idiopathic intracranial hypertension, but it is a typical association. Initial&#x000a0;symptoms include vision changes and headaches. Papilledema may be present on physical examination. Computed tomography and brain&#x000a0;magnetic resonance imaging&#x000a0;may be performed, but the definitive diagnostic test is the measurement of the&#x000a0;cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure upon lumbar puncture.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r39">[39]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Meta-analyses of studies evaluating the efficacy of behavioral and family-based interventions in reducing childhood obesity have shown encouraging outcomes in preschool children. The reduction of screen time has emerged as a&#x000a0;critical&#x000a0;strategy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>Early diagnosis and appropriate&#x000a0;intervention lead to a decline in comorbidities and&#x000a0;an improved overall prognosis. As the burden of complications increases with age and severity,&#x000a0;timely diagnosis and treatment offer the best chance for good health. Clinicians must refer patients promptly for pharmacological and surgical interventions when lifestyle changes and behavioral interventions fail. Childhood obesity often persists into adulthood without appropriate treatment, increasing the risk of long-term adverse health outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Treating pediatric obesity and its comorbid conditions is challenging, time-consuming, and often frustrating for both clinicians and families. Obesity impacts multiple organ systems, most notably the cardiovascular and endocrine systems.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r41">[41]</xref> Dyslipidemia, characterized by low&#x000a0;high-density lipoprotein and high&#x000a0;low-density lipoprotein and total cholesterol, as well as hypertension, increases the risk of future adverse cardiovascular events. Central adiposity, in particular, causes metabolic imbalances that lead to insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome, and it is commonly associated with PCOS. Excess adipose tissue can also interfere with the liver's metabolic and synthetic functions, contributing to the development of MASLD. Obesity is also associated with an increased risk&#x000a0;of gallstones.</p>
        <p>Orthopedic problems are more common when increased pressure from excess body weight affects growing bones. Blount disease and SCFE are likely multifactorial, but obesity frequently contributes to their development. Patients with Blount disease typically present with knee pain, a limp, and severe bowing of the tibiae. SCFE should be suspected when a child presents with hip pain and decreased internal limb rotation on examination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r42">[42]</xref></p>
        <p>Pulmonary&#x000a0;comorbidities include asthma and OSA. Children with asthma may report shortness of breath, cough, and exercise intolerance, while those with&#x000a0;OSA may exhibit symptoms such as snoring, restlessness, daytime sleepiness, and behavioral problems. Pulmonary function tests and polysomnography may be&#x000a0;warranted for further evaluation.</p>
        <p>The psychological complications of obesity can significantly impact a child's daily life and should be addressed early. Interviewing children nonjudgmentally with open-ended questions can encourage them to share any concerns they may have. Additionally, clinicians should inquire about symptoms of anxiety or depression, worsening grades, or experiences of bullying at school. Professional counseling may be necessary to prevent the progression of psychological symptoms.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Pediatric obesity&#x000a0;is a&#x000a0;complex problem that often requires an interprofessional team approach. Failure to adequately diagnose and treat&#x000a0;this condition results in comorbid medical conditions and increases the likelihood of obesity in adulthood. The primary care&#x000a0;clinician usually makes the initial diagnosis, but the subsequent management involves consultation with specialists in a staged manner.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>Stage 1, "Prevention Plus," involves recommendations from primary care clinicians. Children with a BMI between the 85th and 95th percentile are considered overweight. Nutritional advice should include increasing the intake of fruits and vegetables, reducing consumption of sugary drinks and fast foods, and maintaining regular, balanced meals. Clinicians should also assess screen time, sleep, and physical activity habits. Regular follow-up, typically within 3&#x000a0;to 6 months, is essential to monitor progress and make necessary adjustments.</p>
        <p>Failure to improve in 3 to 6 months or a BMI in the obese range warrants consideration of Stage 2, structured weight management, which involves referral to a registered dietitian or nutrition specialist. The recommendations from Stage 1 are continued, but with more structured guidelines for meals and snacks. Motivational interviewing may also be beneficial. Close follow-up is essential to monitor progress. For families experiencing food insecurity, referrals to community resources, such as the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP), food pantries, and soup kitchens, should be provided.</p>
        <p>In Stage 3, a comprehensive&#x000a0;interprofessional team is involved, including dietitians, behavioral counselors, exercise specialists, and social workers who coordinate care with the primary care clinician. This stage focuses on intensive behavioral therapy and frequent follow-up visits to ensure progress.</p>
        <p>Stage 4 involves referral to a tertiary care center with a pediatric weight management program.&#x000a0;Pharmacological and surgical interventions in these centers may be considered if necessary.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Family-based interventions have proven successful and sustainable in managing pediatric obesity. Educating families about healthy eating and exercise is a key factor in achieving positive outcomes. Families should be encouraged to prioritize healthy foods, minimize processed&#x000a0;foods and high-calorie, sugary snacks, while increasing their intake of fruits and vegetables to at least&#x000a0;5 servings per day.&#x000a0;The importance of creating a positive food environment should be emphasized.</p>
        <p>Healthy habits include eating breakfast daily, having regular family meals, and limiting meals and snacks outside the home. Parents should be discouraged from using food as a reward for their children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r44">[44]</xref> Families should also engage in enjoyable physical activities tailored to the child's abilities. Involving caregivers in setting goals related to diet and exercise improves the effectiveness of these interventions. Caregivers can select food options that align with their cultural traditions and food preferences.</p>
        <p>However, financial difficulties may lead parents to work multiple jobs to support their families, which can be an obstacle to family-based interventions. Food insecurity may force families to purchase higher-calorie, less nutritious foods. Unsupervised children are more likely to consume processed or fast foods, spend more time on screens, and engage in less physical activity. Additionally, some neighborhoods are unsafe for outdoor play and physical exercise. Clinicians must consider the child's home environment and any potential barriers to following medical recommendations to take a patient-centered approach to managing pediatric obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131531.r45">[45]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>The 2025 Global Commission's&#x000a0;guidelines may have unintended consequences for young people. Normative ranges for waist circumference or waist-to-hip ratio have not been validated for children, and the new criteria do not account for a child's growth or the role of BMI percentiles by age and sex. Patients currently identified as having obesity may no longer meet the criteria if they have yet to develop signs of organ dysfunction, which often do not appear until age 9 or older. Classifying these children as &#x0201c;preclinical&#x0201d; raises concern that payers may use this designation to deny necessary coverage for obesity-related care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Obesity is a serious public health issue in the U.S. and other countries, with a concerning increase in prevalence over the past few decades.&#x000a0;Primary obesity arises from an imbalance between energy intake and expenditure. Secondary causes, such as endocrine problems, genetic conditions, and head trauma, are less common but must be ruled out before prescribing treatment.</p>
        <p>Primary care clinicians typically diagnose obesity using the patient&#x02019;s history, physical examination, and growth chart review. However, comprehensive management requires collaboration with an interprofessional team that may include nurses, dietitians, nutrition specialists, psychologists, behavioral counselors, exercise specialists, endocrinologists, neurologists, podiatrists, and surgeons. Nurses and nurse practitioners play a&#x000a0;crucial role, especially in pediatric primary care, by educating and motivating families and children to adopt healthier habits.</p>
        <p>Children with obesity require regular monitoring of height, weight, and BMI. Dietitians guide families in selecting low-energy-dense, balanced dietary options. Exercise specialists provide age-appropriate recommendations for physical activity and active play. Psychological issues often lead to maladaptive eating behaviors, with obesity frequently linked to depression, anxiety, and behavior problems. Nurses, mental health professionals, and behavioral counselors within school settings play a key role in identifying and addressing these concerns.</p>
        <p>The interprofessional team's expertise in pediatric weight management programs is fundamental to tertiary care consultations. When behavioral and pharmacologic interventions fail, pediatric or bariatric surgeons assess which children may benefit from bariatric procedures. Consultations with endocrinology, neurology, and genetics may be necessary when secondary causes of obesity are suspected.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131531.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
